[size=16pt][b]Mined Transaction Corpus — consensus acceptance from real chain history[/b][/size]

Seventeen real transactions taken from the main chain, each replayed against the script flags of [i]its own block[/i]. The full-archive replay, reduced to a test that runs in milliseconds.

[b]17[/b] transactions · [b]26 KB[/b] of vectors · 8,695 bytes of transaction across 32 resolved inputs · heights [b]170[/b]–[b]850,000[/b] · [b]17/17[/b] accepted, 68 checks, 0 failures

Every transaction here was mined into the main chain, so each is consensus-valid [i]under the flags active at its own height[/i]. That is the entire claim, and it is deliberately narrow. They were chosen because they broke someone's assumption, not because they are common — a thousand ordinary P2PKH spends would test less than the eleven odd ones below.

[hr]

[size=12pt][b]Scope, stated up front[/b][/size]

This asserts [b]consensus[/b] acceptance only. It does [b]not[/b] assert mempool standardness. Several of these are consensus-valid and [i]non-standard today[/i] — bare multisig, uncompressed keys, high-S signatures — and Core cannot be asked for a policy verdict on a transaction whose inputs are long spent. Conflating the two would produce a confident wrong answer, so policy is tested separately.

[hr]

[size=12pt][b]1. The transactions[/b][/size]

Height is load-bearing, not decoration. DERSIG activates at 363,725, CLTV at 388,381, CSV at 419,328, segwit at 481,824, taproot at 709,632; P2SH took effect at 173,805. A transaction from 2011 must be judged by 2011's rules, and two of these are rejected outright under today's.

"Spends" is the script type of the [i]inputs[/i] — what the verifier actually had to execute. It is not always what the transaction is famous for, and that distinction turned out to matter.

